// Mixed Int/Double arithmetic widens the Int — on the executor and on the bridge alike.
//
// This fixture exists because the two lanes DISAGREED. Until 2026-08-11 the executor refused
// every line below (`Mul on Int 1 and Double 2`) while the bridge computed all of them, so the
// same program printed numbers on one lane and died on the other — invariant I-3. A fixture in
// this directory is the right guard precisely because `exec-gate.sh` runs it BOTH ways and
// compares, so a future one-lane fix cannot pass here.
//
// Both operand orders for all four operators: the executor's arms are homogeneous, so `Int op
// Double` and `Double op Int` miss them independently and one can be fixed without the other.
println(1 + 2.0)
println(2.0 + 1)
println(3 - 0.5)
println(0.5 - 3)
println(1 * 2.0)
println(2.0 * 1)
println(7 / 2.0)
println(2.0 / 8)

// The shape the shared bench wrapper's last line has on every column — an Int REP COUNTER times
// a Double. It is here as a fixture and not only as a bench detail because this is the form that
// found the gap: `_ssc_reps * 1000000.0` is what stopped the wrapper from running on v3 at all.
var reps = 4
println(reps * 1000000.0)
val ns = 250000000
println(ns / 1000000.0)

// `%` is NOT here on purpose. Every lane refuses a Double remainder — native `TYPEERR: % requires
// Int left operand`, interp `No method '%' on Double`, v3 `Rem on Double` — so widening it would
// claim support no lane has. Mixed COMPARISON is absent for the opposite reason: there the lanes
// disagree with each other (interp says `1 < 2.0` is true, native and v2 refuse it at type-check
// time), and a fixture may not quietly pick a side of a divergence.
